etter"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 33\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>33\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 29\u00b0F, and the record is 50\u00b0F (1967). The low of <strong>21\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Anchorage has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Anchorage's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Anchorage has set <strong class=\"sig\">34 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 132 that chance alone would give, and <strong>179 record highs</strong>, more than the 132 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (95). Anchorage's all-time high is 90\u00b0F, on July 4, 2019.","t_chg":"Anchorage has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 72 complete years: Anchorage's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.77\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.55 to +1.00), about +5.5\u00b0F since 1954. Anchorage's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.47\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.25 to +0.68), about +3.3\u00b0F since 1954.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 11 of 12 months. Highs rose in 6 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Anchorage has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Anchorage's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.58 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.20 to +0.96), about +4.1 inches since 1954. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 16.4 inches a year. The wettest year was 2022 (28.60 in); the driest, 1969 (8.08 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 2 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}